---
/**
 * foodcraft / TrustBM — "Tahanan Cafe"'s ruled fact strip.
 *
 * ROLE. The four-up strip the design sets 56px under the hero, on the plain
 * WHITE ground with no panel and no fill behind it. It is drawn entirely in
 * RULES: one hairline across the top, one across the bottom, and a vertical
 * hairline down the right of every cell — the design's grid is made of borders,
 * not gaps, and this strip is the clearest statement of that. Each cell is a
 * 29px WALNUT Libre Caslon figure over a 10px tracked-caps label, centred.
 * It is the first thing read after the headline and the band most likely to be
 * believed, so not one word in it is written by this component.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (both declared by components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `trust.cells` list spec — live in the sidebar with add / remove /
 * reorder, maxItems 4):
 *   trust.cells.${i}.num    — the walnut figure. set:html, so an owner's
 *                             "&lt; 1 hr", "7am", "₱—" or a real <em> emphasis
 *                             survives escaping.
 *   trust.cells.${i}.label  — the tracked-caps label under it.
 * There is no other hook here. A data-field on any path that schema does not
 * declare is a dead hook that no sidebar input owns.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed, never authored by this file):
 *   · `rows` — the cells that actually carry text, each carrying its ORIGINAL
 *     array index `i` so a data-field always addresses the real slot. Dropping
 *     a blank cell 2 must never redirect an edit of cell 3 into it (R6). The
 *     reveal stagger uses the DISPLAY index instead, because it is decoration.
 *   · `--cols` — the column count follows the DATA, capped at the design's
 *     four. The vertical hairlines are drawn per CELL (border-right on the
 *     item), not per track, so a hardcoded repeat(4) would NOT print empty
 *     ruled boxes — an empty grid track paints nothing at all. What it would
 *     do is quieter and still wrong: the strip's top and bottom rules sit on
 *     the CONTAINER and span the whole wrap whatever the cell count, so two
 *     facts would sit crushed into the left quarter of a 1160px rule with the
 *     right half blank — a table whose last entries went missing. The track
 *     count follows what was typed so the rules always frame content.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, and why:
 *   · NO eyebrow, NO headline, NO lede, NO button, NO photo. The design's TRUST
 *     section has none of them and the schema declares none for `trust`. In
 *     particular there is no .kick here, so the family's underlined-eyebrow
 *     signature is simply absent from this strip — as drawn.
 *   · NO placeholder figure, NO zero, NO em dash, NO "—". The demo's "4 /
 *     Philippine farms · 4.9 / Google rating" is the DESIGNER filling their own
 *     template; a farm count or a review score is a claim only the café may
 *     make. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts carries no `trust` key at all, so
 *     this strip exists only where a human typed.
 *   · NO section at all when nothing was typed (R5). The gate counts BODY
 *     content — cells with real text — not a tag or a headline, because this
 *     strip has neither to miscount. lib/astro-builder.ts already forces
 *     visibility.trustBlock false on a zero-length array; this gate is the belt
 *     to that brace, catching what the builder cannot see: rows that are
 *     PRESENT but blank, since the editor's Add button inserts
 *     { num: '', label: '' } and the builder gates on raw array LENGTH. A blank
 *     row surviving would print a ruled cell containing nothing.
 *   · NO onPage() anchor-remap helper, and that is deliberate rather than an
 *     oversight: this strip emits no <a> at all, so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS
 *     (menu / about / gallery / visit / faq / main) would be unreachable dead
 *     code in this one file, and an unused const is what a reviewer would flag
 *     instead. The remap belongs in every BM section that does emit an href.
 *     TrustBL and TrustBK made the same call for the same reason.
 *   · NO role="img", NO .plate, NO .cap — there is no image slot in this strip,
 *     so there is nothing to name (R9). Nothing here is aria-hidden either: the
 *     figure and the label are both real owner content and are announced
 *     normally, and there is no decorative numeral or icon to suppress.
 *   · NO star row, NO rating — this strip carries no review, so R2's rating
 *     rule has nothing to bind to here. If an owner types "4.9" into `num` it
 *     is printed as the TEXT they typed, never re-read as a score and never
 *     redrawn as five glyphs.
 *   · NO band-dark. The closing CTA band is the only dark ground in this
 *     design; this strip sits on white.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for a mistake:
 *
 *   · The RIGHT-HAND hairline on the LAST cell is the design's. Its sc-for puts
 *     border-right on every repeated cell, so the strip closes with a short
 *     vertical rule at the wrap's right edge while the first cell has no rule
 *     on its left. That asymmetry is drawn, and it is reproduced verbatim
 *     rather than "tidied" into a border-left-except-first, which is a
 *     different drawing. Nothing clips it, because unlike the previous
 *     template's slab this strip has no radius and no overflow to hide.
 *
 *   · The section's spacing is `padding: 56px 0 0`, not the design's
 *     `margin: 56px auto 0`. Visually identical — the strip has no ground of
 *     its own — but a padding cannot collapse out through <main> on a site
 *     whose hero block is switched off, and it cannot merge with the next
 *     section's top space. Zero at the BOTTOM is also the design: the space
 *     under the strip belongs to ABOUT's own 84px. The wrapper's shared
 *     `section { padding: clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0 }` is (0,1,1) and this scoped
 *     class compiles to (0,2,0) under Astro's DEFAULT `scopedStyleStrategy:
 *     "attribute"` (astro.config.mjs does not set it), so it wins without
 *     !important. Checked by compiling this file: the emitted selector is
 *     `.th-facts[data-astro-cid-…]`.
 *
 *   · The strip's WIDTH is the wrapper's `.wrap`, not the design's own
 *     `max-width:1160px; padding:0 30px`. EVERY section in the design carries
 *     that same pair and PageBM expresses it once, as `.wrap` =
 *     `min(1160px, 92vw)` — so past ~1260px the ruled strip measures the full
 *     1160 rather than the design's 1100 inner column. Re-cutting the 30px
 *     gutter in this one file would inset this strip from the hero above and
 *     the about block below, and these rules READ as alignment: a hairline
 *     that stops short of the columns it divides is the worse infidelity.
 *     If the gutter is wanted it belongs in the wrapper, once.
 *
 *   · The FIGURE restates only its SIZE. .fig already gives Libre Caslon Text
 *     400, tabular lining numerals, line-height 1 and --accent — which is
 *     exactly the design's #7A4E2D at exactly its line-height. Only the flat
 *     29px is local. Nothing is re-coloured: walnut on white is 7.11:1.
 *
 *   · The LABEL restates its WEIGHT, and this is the one value that departs
 *     from the primitive, so here is the reasoning. .mono is 10px / .2em /
 *     uppercase / var(--sans) / var(--muted) — every one of those is the
 *     design's label verbatim, including the colour (#6B6057 = --muted, 6.11:1
 *     on white — PageBM's header table says 5.71, which understates it; the
 *     whole of that table is a little off and none of it unsafely). The design
 *     draws this particular label at font-weight 600, one notch below the
 *     wrapper's 700 tracked-caps default, and it does so for EVERY quiet caps
 *     label it sets — the footer bar, the gallery caption, the review byline,
 *     the spec and hours values are all 600 — while keeping 700 for the
 *     eyebrows and the buttons. Karla ships 600 and the wrapper loads it.
 *     The design is the specification, so the 600 is reproduced. No other
 *     property of .mono is restated — a section restating a primitive is how a
 *     family drifts apart, and the size/tracking/colour need no help.
 *
 *   · The label's leading is PINNED at 1.3. The design sets no line-height on
 *     its caps labels, so in the design document they compute to `normal`;
 *     PageBM's `body { line-height: 1.78 }` would otherwise inflate this 10px
 *     line box and print the design's 10px gap under the figure as ~14px, and
 *     the cell several px taller than drawn. 1.3 restores the rhythm and still
 *     lets a longer owner label wrap without the two lines' caps touching.
 *
 *   · An owner's <em> inside `num` is LEFT ITALIC and left WALNUT. The italic
 *     emphasis is this template's signature and Libre Caslon Text ships a real
 *     400 italic, so nothing is synthesised. It inherits --accent from .fig for
 *     free: `:where(.h2 em)` and `:where(.em)` cannot reach a bare <em> inside
 *     a .fig, and the browser default supplies the slant. NO rule is written
 *     for it, so no :global() is needed — had one been, set:html output sits
 *     outside scoped CSS and would have required it (R3).
 *
 *   · Both cell values carry `overflow-wrap: anywhere`. A pasted URL or an
 *     unspaced string in a 29px serif is the one thing in this strip that could
 *     push the page sideways at 520 (R8). Nothing here uses a clamp at all, so
 *     no floor in the 900 query can outrank a cap in the 520 query.
 *
 *   · The strip TOLERATES two content shapes. The schema stores
 *     `content.trust = { cells: [...] }`; a few older records store a plain
 *     array at `content.trust`. Both are read, and the data-field path is
 *     written as the schema's `trust.cells.${i}.*` either way, because that is
 *     the only path the sidebar owns.
 *
 * No id: nothing on the page links to this strip (the BM anchor set is menu,
 * about, gallery, visit, faq, main).
 *
 * Class names are prefixed th-facts- for THIS template. They are not the
 * previous template's names re-used: an audit had to rename a whole set that
 * had been copied verbatim between two files in this family.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const trust = content?.trust || {};
// Tolerate both shapes at every read: the schema's { cells: [...] } and the
// legacy plain array some older records still store under content.trust.
const cells: any[] = Array.isArray(trust.cells)
  ? trust.cells
  : (Array.isArray(trust) ? trust : []);

const asText = (v: any) => (v === null || v === undefined ? '' : String(v));

// The ORIGINAL index rides along in `i`. Only rows with real text survive, so a
// half-filled row prints one line and an empty row prints no cell at all — no
// vertical hairline dividing a column that is not there (R2, R6).
const rows = cells
  .map((c: any, i: number) => ({ value: asText(c?.num), note: asText(c?.label), i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.value.trim() || r.note.trim()));

// Follow the data, but never widen past the design's four tracks (the schema
// caps the list at 4 anyway). Anything beyond four wraps to a second row and
// picks up a horizontal hairline above it.
const cols = String(Math.min(Math.max(rows.length, 1), 4));
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="th-facts">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="th-facts-strip" style={`--cols:${cols}`}>
        {rows.map((r, k) => (
          <div class="th-facts-item reveal" data-delay={String(k % 4)}>
            {r.value.trim() && (
              <div class="fig th-facts-value" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.num`} set:html={r.value}></div>
            )}
            {r.note.trim() && (
              <div class="mono th-facts-note" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.label`}>{r.note}</div>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from the wrapper's token vocabulary
     (--line --accent --muted --disp --sans, via .wrap / .fig / .mono). There is
     not one literal hex in this file (R1).

     (Never write a literal end-style-tag inside a comment in here — the parser
      would end the stylesheet on the spot and silently truncate the file.) */

  /* The design's TRUST section has no vertical padding of its own — the cells
     do all of it — and it sits 56px under the hero with nothing beneath it.
     See the frontmatter note on why that 56px is padding rather than margin. */
  .th-facts { padding: 56px 0 0; }

  /* The strip: a grid drawn with RULES. One hairline over, one under, and the
     tracks follow the data up to the design's four. */
  .th-facts-strip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 1), minmax(0, 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  /* 28px 22px, centred, with the design's hairline on the right of EVERY cell,
     including the last, which closes the strip at the wrap's edge. */
  .th-facts-item {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 28px 22px;
    text-align: center;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  /* Only reachable if a record predates the schema's maxItems 4 cap. */
  .th-facts-item:nth-child(n + 5) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }

  /* .fig already gives Libre Caslon Text 400, tabular lining numerals,
     line-height 1 and the design's walnut. The flat 29px is the only local
     value. Written as a descendant so it clears the wrapper's (0,1,1) rules
     with room to spare, whatever the source order. */
  .th-facts-strip .th-facts-value {
    font-size: 29px;
    /* A pasted URL or an unspaced string must not push the page sideways. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* .mono is already 10px / .2em / uppercase / var(--sans) / var(--muted) —
     the design's label treatment exactly, colour included. Three things are
     local, and only three:
       · font-weight 600 — the design draws this label one notch below the
         wrapper's 700 tracked-caps default; see the frontmatter note.
       · margin-top: 10px — the design's gap under the figure.
       · line-height: 1.3 — the design's caps labels set no leading, so they
         compute to `normal` in the design document. PageBM's body 1.78 would
         leak in, inflate this 10px line box and print that 10px gap as ~14px. */
  .th-facts-strip .th-facts-note {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 10px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── This section's OWN responsive pass. The wrapper handles the shared
        760 / 520 type pass; the grid below is ours (R8). Nothing here uses a
        clamp, so there is no floor in one query that can outgrow a cap in the
        next. ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Two up. The interior vertical rule is now the odd cell's right border and
       every cell past the first pair gains a horizontal one. The horizontal
       rule is restated rather than left to lapse: compiled it reads
       .th-facts-item[data-astro-cid]:nth-child(n+3) — (0,3,0), which beats the
       base cell's (0,2,0) whatever the source order, but a reader should not
       have to work that out. */
    .th-facts-strip { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .th-facts-item:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* One column. A 10px label at .2em tracking cannot hold a ~180px half
       column without splitting mid-word. The trailing right hairline is dropped
       here: at full width it would draw a stray vertical line down the page
       edge instead of dividing anything, and the horizontal rule between cells
       takes over the dividing.

       ONLY the VERTICAL padding moves off the design's 28px 22px, and only
       because stacking multiplies it: four cells one above the other spend the
       design's vertical padding four times over. The 22px HORIZONTAL padding is
       the design's and stays — nothing at this width is pressing on it (the
       wrap is 92vw, so the cell is ~390px wide).

       The figure stays at the design's flat 29px. It is NOT shrunk: the cell is
       WIDER one-up at 520 than two-up at 900, where the figure is left alone,
       so a 520-only reduction would be both a deviation and backwards. Long
       unbroken strings are already handled by overflow-wrap above. */
    .th-facts-strip { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .th-facts-item {
      padding: 22px;
      border-right: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    }
    .th-facts-item:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
